/Pg 15 0 R
/K [21]
>>
endobj
282 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 120 0 R
/E (Now let's introduce an LLM that makes HandyWorker equally good at coding as DevWorker:)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [22]
>>
endobj
283 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 501 0 R
/E (Now HandyWorker can produce either 20 stuffs per day or 20 codes per day)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [23]
>>
endobj
284 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 502 0 R
/E (DevWorker isn't affected by the LLM since he was already a master coder: he still produces either 10 stuffs per day or 20 codes per day)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [24]
>>
endobj
285 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 120 0 R
/E (If we now try to work out the possible mutually beneficial trade scenarios, we realize that the 1:1 exchange rate between stuff and code no longer works. This is because HandyWorker can make that exchange rate internally. If DevWorker offered to trade 10 code for 10 stuff as in the previous scenario, HandyWorker would say "No" because he can just do that himself without trading.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [25 26]
>>
endobj
286 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 120 0 R
/E (In fact, if we work out the math, we can show that the set of mutually beneficial trades has actually shrunk for DevWorker as shown in the below graphs:)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [27]
>>
endobj
